<p>The first four levels are the <em>structural</em> hierarchy: a suite holds groups, a group holds tests,
  and a test expands into cases. Flows and steps are the <em>procedural</em> hierarchy: they are what a
  case body actually does. A case may call flows, a flow calls steps, and a step is the unit that can be
  dispatched to a remote endpoint.</p>

<p>Every level is optional above and below the one you need. A single test file with a <code>main()</code>
  runs on its own without a group or a suite, and a case that only needs a few lines of Python does not
  have to be split into flows and steps at all. The pages in this section follow that order of
  dependence: the building blocks first - configuration fields, statuses and result fields - then the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/test/">test</a> that uses them, and outward from there to
  steps, flows, endpoints, groups and suites.</p>

<h2 id="package">Orcraft package</h2>
<p>The Orcraft test framework is the plain-Python library your tests are written against. It ships with
  the controller and is imported from a single package:</p>
{{ codeblock.block('from orcraft import (
	OrcraftTest,
	OrcraftTestConfig,
	OrcraftTestCase,
	OrcraftConfigField,
)') }}

<p>There is no domain-specific language, no runner conventions and no decorators to memorize. A test is a
  Python class that inherits from a framework base class, declares its configuration as class attributes,
  and implements one method. Everything else - discovery, configuration, distribution to endpoints,
  execution, logging and result collection - is what the controller does with that class.</p>

<p>Because a suite is ordinary Python, the same files run three ways without modification: standalone
  from the command line, from the controller GUI, and from the controller CLI in a CI pipeline. The
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/">Tutorial</a> walks through building one step by step; this
  section is the reference for what the framework itself offers.</p>

<h2 id="three-classes">Config, component, result</h2>
<p>Whatever the level, a component is always three classes - a config that declares its inputs, the
  component that holds its behavior, and a result that declares its outputs:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('class MyTestConfig(OrcraftTestConfig):
	name = OrcraftConfigField.Text(default="My Test")
	payload_size = OrcraftConfigField.Numeric(default=64, label="Payload Size")


class MyTestResult(OrcraftTestResult):
	measured_value = OrcraftResultField.Numeric(default=0, label="Measured Value")


class MyTest(OrcraftTest):
	def __init__(self, config: MyTestConfig | None = None) -> None:
		super().__init__(config or MyTestConfig())

	def _run_case(self, case: OrcraftTestCase):
		case.result = MyTestResult()
		case.result.measured_value = measure(self.config.payload_size)
		return case.result') }}

<p>Those declarations are not just Python defaults. The controller reads them to build the run
  configuration panel in the GUI, to validate that required values are supplied before a run starts, and
  to render the config and result tables in the log. A field added to a config class appears in the GUI
  with no further wiring.</p>

